1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,496
Hello! Last time we learned the eight simple vowels — 'ㅏ' [a], 'ㅓ' [eo], 'ㅗ' [o], 'ㅜ' [u], 'ㅡ' [eu], 'ㅣ' [i], 'ㅐ' [ae], 'ㅔ' [e].

2
00:00:06,846 --> 00:00:11,910
Today we'll build whole words from those vowels alone — not a single consonant.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:18,248
In front of Gwanghwamun, the changing of the guard happens twice a day.

4
00:00:18,598 --> 00:00:22,462
They march to the drum, spears upright, holding their posts.

5
00:00:22,812 --> 00:00:27,588
See the guard walking towards us? He starts tiny, far away, and grows.

6
00:00:27,938 --> 00:00:32,402
The closer someone comes, the bigger they look. That is perspective.

7
00:00:32,752 --> 00:00:36,088
A dot in the distance — and now you can see his face.

8
00:00:36,438 --> 00:00:39,726
Today we'll walk this square and pick up words as we go.

9
00:00:40,076 --> 00:00:44,965
When we write, a small circle 'ㅇ' [ng] sits in front — but it makes no sound at all.

10
00:00:45,315 --> 00:00:49,371
We've reached Gwanghwamun. Shall we meet today's first word?

11
00:00:49,721 --> 00:00:54,544
'아이' [a-i] — it means a child. 'ㅏ' [a] and 'ㅣ' [i], two vowels sitting side by side.

12
00:00:54,894 --> 00:00:57,776
'아' [a] — '이' [i]. Slowly now, join them. '아이' [a-i].

13
00:01:02,076 --> 00:01:06,060
Shall we step down one at a time? One step, one sound.

14
00:01:06,410 --> 00:01:10,540
'이' [i] — a single letter is already a word. It's the tooth in your mouth.

15
00:01:10,890 --> 00:01:13,612
Pull your lips wide to the sides and you get '이' [i].

16
00:01:13,962 --> 00:01:18,489
'ㅏ' [a] and 'ㅣ' [i] both stand upright — shaped after a person standing tall.

17
00:01:18,839 --> 00:01:24,181
Look up the steps — '위' [wi]. 'ㅜ' [u] and 'ㅣ' [i] joined into one letter. It means above.

18
00:01:24,531 --> 00:01:29,187
King Sejong looks down from the top of these steps — the man who made Hangeul.

19
00:01:29,537 --> 00:01:33,521
Down the last step. Shall we head for the tunnel made of water?

20
00:01:44,076 --> 00:01:49,500
Gwanghwamun Square has a tunnel of water — seventy-seven jets arching overhead.

21
00:01:49,850 --> 00:01:53,930
Stickman runs to the far end and back — smaller going, bigger returning.

22
00:01:54,280 --> 00:01:58,336
Walk on through the jets — the next words wait at the stall ahead.

23
00:02:00,076 --> 00:02:05,129
Oh — something's rolling this way. Today's second word: '오이' [o-i], a green cucumber.

24
00:02:05,479 --> 00:02:10,299
Round your lips for '오' [o], then pull them wide for '이' [i]. '오' [o] — '이' [i]. '오이' [o-i].

25
00:02:10,649 --> 00:02:15,846
'아이' [a-i] and '오이' [o-i] — same ending. Change one sound in front and the meaning changes.

26
00:02:16,195 --> 00:02:19,171
The long green one — that's the cucumber you eat.

27
00:02:19,521 --> 00:02:23,747
There's a tiny baby by the stall. A very young child is a '유아' [yu-a].

28
00:02:24,097 --> 00:02:27,347
'ㅠ' [yu] and 'ㅏ' [a] — vowels only again, no consonant.

29
00:02:27,697 --> 00:02:32,545
Vowels can stand alone with no consonant — rare among writing systems.

30
00:02:32,895 --> 00:02:37,263
Put two vowels side by side and you hear both sounds, one after the other.

31
00:02:37,613 --> 00:02:41,789
Both letters are the same size — Hangeul is written to fit a square block.

32
00:02:48,076 --> 00:02:51,892
Wait — something's tipping over on the right side of the stall.

33
00:02:52,242 --> 00:02:55,471
Careful — the milk is falling! '우유' [u-yu] — sweet white milk.

34
00:02:55,821 --> 00:03:00,102
Push your lips forward for '우' [u], then '유' [yu]. '우' [u] — '유' [yu]. '우유' [u-yu].

35
00:03:00,452 --> 00:03:03,887
Why pick it up so fast? That reason is '이유' [i-yu].

36
00:03:04,237 --> 00:03:07,544
'ㅣ' [i] and 'ㅠ' [yu] — '이유' [i-yu] is made of two vowels too.

37
00:03:08,076 --> 00:03:12,483
Whoa! The fountain shoots up. The sound you make when startled — '오' [o]!

38
00:03:12,833 --> 00:03:15,750
'오' [o] — just one letter, and a real word all the same.

39
00:03:16,100 --> 00:03:20,072
And '오' [o] also means five. One, two, three, four, five — '오' [o]!

40
00:03:20,422 --> 00:03:24,230
For a bigger surprise — '우와' [u-wa]. Wow. Vowels only again.

41
00:03:27,076 --> 00:03:32,116
Here stands the Hangeul Fountain — two hundred twenty-five floor nozzles

42
00:03:32,466 --> 00:03:37,028
Heaven 'ㆍ' [a], Earth 'ㅡ' [eu], Human 'ㅣ' [i] — these three shapes make every vowel.

43
00:03:37,378 --> 00:03:41,818
Fourteen consonants, ten vowels, and four old letters no longer used today.

44
00:03:42,168 --> 00:03:44,928
Araea, Bansiot, Yet-ieung, Yeorin-hieut.

45
00:03:45,278 --> 00:03:49,574
They rise and scatter like water. Letters change with the times.

46
00:03:49,924 --> 00:03:53,284
The square also hosts taekwondo demonstrations.

47
00:03:53,634 --> 00:03:59,106
A flying side kick, a 540 spinning kick, and four boards in one jump.

48
00:03:59,456 --> 00:04:02,904
Doing it outdoors like this is '야외' [ya-oe] — 'ㅑ' [ya] and 'ㅚ' [oe].

49
00:04:03,254 --> 00:04:06,902
Stickman cheers from among the crowd, hand in the air.

50
00:04:07,252 --> 00:04:10,642
Shouting in disapproval is '야유' [ya-yu] — 'ㅑ' [ya] and 'ㅠ' [yu].

51
00:04:10,992 --> 00:04:16,124
Someone's on the bench. Sixth word — '아우' [a-u], a warm word for a younger sibling.

52
00:04:16,474 --> 00:04:20,598
From 'ㅏ' [a] to 'ㅜ' [u]. Open wide, then round forward. '아' [a] — '우' [u].

53
00:04:20,948 --> 00:04:26,642
Sounds have a temperature too. 'ㅏ' [a] and 'ㅗ' [o] are bright and warm; 'ㅓ' [eo] and 'ㅜ' [u] are darker.

54
00:04:26,992 --> 00:04:32,200
Same temperature, and they snap together like magnets — warm with warm, cool with cool.

55
00:04:32,550 --> 00:04:37,419
Only 'ㅣ' [i] is the exception — shaped after a standing person, it gets along with both.

56
00:04:37,769 --> 00:04:40,553
Let's sit side by side and match our mouths.

57
00:04:40,903 --> 00:04:44,839
Copy my mouth. See — my friend beside me is copying too.

58
00:04:46,992 --> 00:04:51,448
Shh… something's in the bush. Seventh word — '여우' [yeo-u], the red fox.

59
00:04:51,798 --> 00:04:54,112
Oh, it's gone. '여' [yeo] — '우' [u]. '여우' [yeo-u].

60
00:04:54,462 --> 00:04:59,262
'아야' [a-ya] — ouch! A tumble on the leaves. That's what you say when it hurts.

61
00:04:59,612 --> 00:05:03,896
Walking slowly under the falling leaves — that calm is '여유' [yeo-yu], leisure.

62
00:05:10,292 --> 00:05:14,468
The sun is going down. The square we walked all day turns red.

63
00:05:14,818 --> 00:05:18,922
Friends gather one by one. Shall we all look up at the sky?

64
00:05:24,292 --> 00:05:29,236
The sun sets, and the lanterns come on one by one, starting from the left.

65
00:05:29,586 --> 00:05:33,618
When the last lantern lights up, we all raise our hands together.

66
00:05:33,968 --> 00:05:37,381
This place we've walked all day — that's '야외' [ya-oe], outdoors.

67
00:05:40,292 --> 00:05:44,012
Take out a mirror. Watch your own mouth as you copy me.

68
00:05:44,362 --> 00:05:48,143
'아' [a], '이' [i], '오' [o], '우' [u]. See how the shape changes each time?

69
00:05:48,493 --> 00:05:53,458
Today's words — '아이' [a-i], '이' [i], '위' [wi], '오이' [o-i], '유아' [yu-a], '우유' [u-yu], '이유' [i-yu].

70
00:05:53,808 --> 00:05:59,877
And '오' [o], '우와' [u-wa], '야외' [ya-oe], '야유' [ya-yu], '아우' [a-u], '여우' [yeo-u], '아야' [a-ya], '여유' [yeo-yu] — fifteen in all.

71
00:06:00,227 --> 00:06:04,619
Every one is built from vowels alone. Not a single consonant.

72
00:06:04,969 --> 00:06:10,638
Next time we'll put the silent 'ㅇ' [ng] in front of a vowel and build syllables — consonants at last.

73
00:06:10,988 --> 00:06:14,372
Thank you for following along. See you next time!
